Keep `flutter_riverpod` 2.6.1 and `autoDispose`. `Ref.mounted` was introduced in Riverpod 3. On 2.6.1, register a lifetime guard with `ref.onDispose` before the fetch starts. When the provider is disposed, invalidate the guard and cancel the request if the client supports cancellation. Check the guard before publishing either data or an error, since cancellation can itself complete the future with an error.

For a fetch started by a notifier method:

```dart
Future<void> fetch() async {
  var disposed = false;
  ref.onDispose(() => disposed = true);

  state = const AsyncLoading();

  final result = await AsyncValue.guard(() => repository.fetch());
  if (disposed) return;

  state = result;
}
```

`AsyncValue.guard` captures both success and failure, so one check protects both paths. If the screen can trigger overlapping fetches, also track which request is current and publish only that request’s result. The lifetime guard handles disposal, while the request identity handles out-of-order completion within the same lifetime.

Verify this with a fake repository backed by a `Completer`:

1. Mount the screen, start the fetch, and confirm it shows loading. Pop the screen, pump until the provider’s disposal callback runs, and confirm cancellation if the client supports it.
2. In separate tests, complete the original future with data and with an error **after disposal**. Pump again and assert that neither completion publishes state or causes an uncaught exception. Let the fake complete even if cancellation was requested so the test exercises the guard.
3. Keep the screen mounted in control tests and verify that success shows data and failure shows the error UI. If the provider can recompute during a fetch, also verify that a completion from the old lifetime cannot overwrite the new one.
